19:35:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question, and the short answer is: **it already happened—kind of—and the internet didn’t break.** But the full story is more nuanced, and it helps to understand what “running out” really means. ### 1. The “Run Out” Happened in Stages The pool of available IPv4 addresses wasn’t one giant bucket. It was managed hierarchically: - **IANA** (Internet Assigned Numbers Authority) doled out huge blocks to the five **Regional Internet Registries** (RIRs). On **31 January 2011**, IANA allocated its last two free /8 blocks (each with ~16.7 million addresses) to APNIC (Asia Pacific), which triggered a global exhaustion event. - **RIRs** then gave out what they had to ISPs and enterprises. The last ones started running dry around 2012–2019: - **APNIC** exhausted its general pool in April 2011. - **RIPE NCC** (Europe, Middle East, parts of Central Asia) exhausted in September 2012. - **LACNIC** (Latin America & Caribbean) in June 2014. - **ARIN** (US, Canada, many Caribbean & North Atlantic islands) in September 2015. - **AFRINIC** (Africa) is the only one that still has a small pool, but even that is dwindling. So for most of the world, **there are no fresh, unallocated IPv4 addresses from the RIRs** to give to new ISPs or companies growing their networks. But that doesn’t mean all addresses are currently in use—just that the register is full. ### 2. What Actually Happened Next? Workarounds, Not Meltdowns Because the exhaustion was predicted years in advance, the Internet engineering community had time to deploy several mitigations. So **nothing catastrophic happened** on the day the pools ran dry. Instead, we just live in a world where: #### **a. Network Address Translation (NAT) Became Even More Pervasive** Most homes and businesses already use NAT, where many devices share a single public IPv4 address. After exhaustion, ISPs often moved to **Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT)**—essentially putting many customers behind a second layer of NAT. This means: - You no longer have your own public IP address; you share one with hundreds of other subscribers. - It’s harder to host services from home, peer-to-peer gaming can break, and you can’t just port-forward. - IPv4 growth is capped: adding new subscribers doesn’t require a new public address, but the quality of connectivity suffers. #### **b. A Thriving IPv4 Transfer Market** Since you can’t get “new” addresses from the RIRs, organizations that have more than they need can sell their address blocks on the open market. Prices have soared—a /16 block (65,536 addresses) now costs hundreds of thousands of dollars. Big cloud providers and CDNs buy them aggressively. So IPv4 addresses became a **tradable, scarce commodity**, which distorts some business models. #### **c. The Slow but Steady Rise of IPv6** IPv6 has a virtually inexhaustible address space (340 undecillion addresses). It was designed precisely to avoid this problem. But adoption has been slow because IPv4 and IPv6 are not directly compatible; you need both protocols running simultaneously (dual-stack) or some translation mechanism. After exhaustion, IPv6 deployment accelerated: - Today, over 40% of Google users access it via IPv6, and major mobile networks (T-Mobile USA, Reliance Jio in India) are almost entirely IPv6-only with IPv4-as-a-service using 464XLAT/NAT64. - However, many legacy systems, cheap IoT devices, and some enterprises still lag behind. #### **d. No Change for the Average User (Mostly)** If you’re a regular home user, you probably didn’t notice a thing. You still get an IP from your ISP, you browse the web, stream videos, etc. Behind the scenes, your traffic might traverse multiple layers of NAT, but that’s transparent for most client-side activities. The problems only become apparent when you try to run your own server, use VPNs that require a public IP, or play certain games. ### 3. Who Feels the Pain? - **New ISPs or those in developing regions**: They can’t get cheap blocks for expanding their customer base; they must buy or accept CGNAT from the start. - **Hosting providers and enterprises** that need consistent public IPs for mail servers, DNS, etc., face rising costs and stricter justification requirements. - **The Internet’s architecture purists**: End-to-end transparency is largely dead for IPv4, making network troubleshooting and innovation harder. - **IoT manufacturers**: Many cheap devices only support IPv4, forcing reliance on NAT and central cloud relays. ### 4. So, Does the Internet Eventually Stop Working? **No.** IPv4 will probably never “run out” completely in the sense that all addresses are actively used and none are returned. Instead, we’re in a long-term transition phase where: - IPv4 becomes a legacy, tunneled-over protocol for reaching the leftovers of the Internet that haven’t upgraded. - IPv6 becomes the primary protocol, with IPv4 available “on demand” via translation (e.g., MAP-T, 464XLAT) only when you need to talk to an old-school server. - Some services may go IPv6-only, forcing even stragglers to upgrade. - The address market will continue, but eventually IPv6 may make IPv4 addressees less valuable. **In essence, running out of IPv4 addresses didn’t kill the internet—it just forced us to grow up and adopt the future protocol we designed 25 years ago.** The sky didn’t fall, but we’re now living with permanent workarounds that sometimes creak, while the slow march to IPv6 continues.
