19:36:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a fair suspicion—after all, if we’re slapping on layers of NAT and trading IP addresses like gold, it sure *feels* like a last-minute scramble. But the reality is the opposite: **the internet engineering community planned for this decades ago, and NAT/CGNAT is actually the unplanned kludge that stuck around too long.** Here’s the real timeline: --- ### 1. The Warning Bell Rang in the Early 1990s By 1992, it was already obvious that the 4.3 billion addresses in IPv4 would be woefully insufficient. The IETF (Internet Engineering Task Force) immediately started working on a next-generation protocol. That project became **IPv6**, with the core specification published as an Internet Standard in **1998**. So the permanent fix—a massive address space with no need for NAT—was fully designed *before* most people had even heard of the internet. ### 2. Meanwhile, a Temporary Band-Aid: NAT While IPv6 was being designed, the internet was growing explosively, and waiting for a global upgrade wasn’t practical. So engineers came up with a short-term workaround: **Network Address Translation (NAT)**. The first RFC describing it is from **1994**. It let many devices share one public address, easing the immediate pressure. The crucial point: **NAT was never intended as a permanent solution.** It broke the end-to-end principle, made peer-to-peer communication hard, and complicated security. But it worked *just well enough* to defer the IPv6 rollout, creating a dependency that became deeply entrenched. ### 3. So Why Didn’t Everyone Just Switch to IPv6 in 1998? Because the internet isn’t a single system you can upgrade with a switch. It’s a patchwork of billions of devices, thousands of ISPs, and millions of networks. For transition to happen, almost every node, router, application, and supporting system had to be updated to speak IPv6 **in addition** to IPv4 (dual-stack), or use complicated translation. The incentives were misaligned: - **ISPs** saw that NAT worked, so why invest in a costly new infrastructure if customers weren’t demanding it? - **Content providers** waited for users to get IPv6 before making their services available over it. - **Users** had no idea any of this existed; their memes, email, and Facebook worked fine. That classic chicken-and-egg problem let inertia win for over a decade. It wasn’t a lack of planning—it was a failure of collective action despite a perfectly good plan sitting on the shelf. ### 4. So What Are We “Scrambling” Now? What you’re seeing today isn’t a scramble to invent a fix. It’s the slow, grinding effort to finally execute the transition plan that was written in the 90s. The “scramble” manifests as: - **CGNAT** being slapped on because ISPs have no more public addresses *and* haven’t fully deploying IPv6 yet. - A booming IPv4 market because networks still refuse to upgrade, so they buy time with expensive legacy addresses. - Enterprises and cloud providers finally moving to IPv6 because the cost of IPv4 (both in money and engineering pain) now exceeds the migration cost. IPv6 is the original, long-term fix—not a patch. NAT and CGNAT are the makeshift patches that kept things limping along, and ironically, they’re now the biggest proof that the planned solution was essential. The “scramble” is just the tipping point where the pain of those patches finally forces the adoption of the protocol that was ready 25 years ago.
