19:37:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an incredibly important nuance, and the IT folks you’ve spoken to are absolutely correct on the ground. Their daily reality is exactly what you describe: no single deadline, no one in charge saying “switch by X date,” just an endless string of workarounds. The disconnect you’re sensing is between **having a technical solution** and **having an orchestrated migration plan with enforcement teeth**. When engineers say “we planned for it,” they mean the protocol. When your IT contacts shrug, they mean the deployment strategy—and that’s where the planning really does fall apart. Let me break down why there’s no “solid plan” from their perspective. --- ### 1. The Internet Has No CEO, No Government, No Project Manager The internet is a federation of independent networks (ISPs, enterprises, cloud providers, etc.) that voluntarily interconnect. There is no central authority that can mandate “All networks must turn on IPv6 by 2028, and shut off IPv4 by 2030.” The IETF can write standards, but it cannot force anyone to implement them. Regional registries allocate addresses, but they don’t control network configurations. Governments could theoretically mandate IPv6 for their own agencies, but not the world. So what passes for a “plan” is actually a bunch of uncoordinated, self-interested decisions made by millions of entities. That’s why your IT people say there’s no solid plan—because in any centrally managed sense, there truly isn’t. ### 2. The “Plan” Was Always: Voluntary, Market-Driven, Gradual Transition The official transition strategy from the 90s was **“dual-stack and gently phase out IPv4 over time once everybody has it.”** The assumption was that as the IPv4 crunch hit, the pain would naturally push everyone to adopt IPv6. That assumption turned out to be naive. Here’s why: - **NAT/CGNAT** extended the life of IPv4 far beyond what anyone expected, delaying the pain. - The **costs and risks** of switching fell on early movers, while the benefits were shared across the whole internet—a classic collective action problem. - Many networks decided it was cheaper to just buy IPv4 addresses on the market or layer on more CGNAT than to upgrade their entire infrastructure. So there was never a coordinated project plan with milestones and accountability. The “plan” was a hope that economic gravity would pull everyone to IPv6, and that hope didn’t account for the ingenuity and laziness that NAT enabled. ### 3. Why IT Folks Deal With It Day-by-Day From their perspective, the internet’s addressing architecture is a massive technical debt that nobody is willing to pay down, and no manager has a budget for “make our network future-proof.” So they cope: - They set up CGNAT boxes and deal with the fallout (port forwarding tickets, broken gaming, complex logging). - They maintain painful spreadsheets of internal IPv4 subnets to reclaim addresses. - They beg for a bigger IPv4 budget from the CFO, who knows what the grey market prices are. - They deploy IPv6 on the side, if they can, only to realize that half the services their users need don’t support it yet, so they still need IPv4 anyway. There’s no end state because for them, **the end state is “when everyone else finally moves,”** but they can’t control that. So they shrug and just keep the lights on. ### 4. Comparing to Y2K Helps Explain the Difference Y2K had a non-negotiable deadline: December 31, 1999. Failure meant systems would literally break on January 1. Every company and government had to fix its own software by a fixed date, and there was massive management attention and budgeting. IPv6 has no such cliff. IPv4 still works; it’s just getting more expensive and creakier. There’s no date when IPv4 will be turned off globally—it will probably never be entirely turned off. Without a hard deadline and immediate catastrophic failure, there’s no top-down pressure to create a mandated plan. So it remains a permanent, low-grade headache rather than a project with a finish line. ### 5. So What Was “Planned”? The engineering community *did* plan an elegant solution (IPv6) and a transition architecture (dual-stack, tunneling, translation). But they completely failed to plan the **social and economic coordination** required to make it happen. There was no Treaty of the Internet, no regulatory body with teeth, no incentive structure, and no marketing campaign for normal people. In that sense, your IT contacts are right: nobody planned for *how to actually get the world to adopt it*. The result is what we have today: a brilliant technical fix sitting in a drawer while the world keeps duct-taping the old system, and frontline IT staff dealing with the mess. The “day-by-day” coping is the real-world plan—decentralized, reactive, and perpetually incomplete.
